      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pikeville takes its name from Nathan Pike, the son of early settler Samuel Pike, who transferred most of his 2,500‑acre grant to him in 1785.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        20,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikeville%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally called Pike’s Junction, the town grew at the crossroads of major routes, becoming a vital meeting point for commerce and transportation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://pikevillenc.gov/historic-pikeville/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Northern Wayne Heritage Museum, located in Pikeville, showcases the county’s tobacco and textile industries and its evolving education system.",
